<h3 id="define-websockets">Define Websockets</h3>

<p>Websockets are a communication protocol that is considered to be an upgrade over HTTP. The main catch with websockets is, unlike
traditional HTTP connections where after a client receives a response from a request it make to a server, the server closes 
the connection. There are other techniques of keeping the connection alive(persisting the connection) but as I studied more 
into Websockets and HTTP protocols, I got to know this ‘keep-alive’ state set for HTTP protocols may have its downsides.</p>

<p>Hope to cover more on that, back to websockets, The entire idea behind websockets is once a connection is created with the server,
there is bi-directional, full-duplex communication link established. This comes with the perk of being low-latent. <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Low latency in
computer networks mean there is minimal delay in processing volumes of data messages.</code> Hence its suitable for online multiplayer
games, chat applications, sports or financial applications where change in data should be readily available to users/clients.</p>

<h3 id="style">Style</h3>

<p>Unlike HTTP, Websockets also have their url scheme which helps identify websocket connections.
An example in Dart/Flutter:</p>

<figure class="highlight"><pre><code class="language-dart" data-lang="dart"><span class="c1">// parse uri</span>
<span class="n">val</span> <span class="n">uri</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="n">Uri</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="na">parse</span><span class="o">(</span><span class="s">"ws://some-realtime-app"</span><span class="o">);</span>
<span class="n">val</span> <span class="n">channel</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="n">IOWebSocketChannel</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="na">connect</span><span class="o">(</span><span class="n">uri</span><span class="o">);</span>

<span class="c1">// send message</span>
<span class="n">channel</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="na">sink</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="na">add</span><span class="o">(</span><span class="s">"some-message-to-server"</span><span class="o">);</span>

<span class="c1">// listen to server</span>
<span class="n">channel</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="na">listen</span><span class="o">((</span><span class="n">event</span><span class="o">){</span> <span class="cm">/* handle some response stream */</span> <span class="o">});</span></code></pre></figure>

<p>This connection established happens over a single TCP connection and attaching event handlers allows you to know the state
of the connection. In Dart/Flutter the web_socket_channel package provides a cross-platform wrapper to use WebSocket connections
on android and iOS devices. Take alook at the package on flutter pub.dev if you want to play around with it.</p>

<h3 id="websockets-usecases">WebSockets Usecases</h3>

<p>With most applications needing real-time data to be consumed by its clients, it’s imperative you understand the usecase of the
project and choose the appropriate technology needed. Below I try to list a couple of usecases needing websockets implementation;</p>

<ul>
  <li>Financial tickers</li>
  <li>Multiplayer games</li>
  <li>Sports updates</li>
  <li>Social Feeds / Messaging / Chat applications</li>
  <li>Location-based apps</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="my-take">My take</h3>

<p>Initially not being familiar with websockets, I intended on building the project that introduced me to websockets by using a
technique called periodic polling, where after a certain duration, the client device makes a request to the server for new data,
if any, the fetch data is updated. Actually this was my initial idea of solving the issue with getting real-time data.</p>

<p>This not only has some downsides like HTTP overhead, constant querying for new data. But also it would take additional time
implementing this periodic scheduling for data. I pretty new this all this and after reading a from a couple of places, I learned
there is other methods like <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Long Polling</code> by which the the connection to the server is kept alive and the server returns 
a ‘new’ response after a certain period.</p>

<p>Looking at websockets, once the connection is establish and messages sent to the user. There will be no need to constantly query 
the server for new data. You just have to setup listener and once new data is available, its sent to its consumers.</p>

<p>To end here, I think Websockets are a cool protocol, Hoping to use it more and experiment with it. See you in the next one.</p>
